  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does rain take salt out of rocks?",
      "a": "Rain picks up a little carbon dioxide from the air, which makes it very weakly acidic. Over time it dissolves minerals such as sodium, calcium and potassium out of rocks, and rivers carry them away.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is the sea salty?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why doesn't evaporation take the salt?",
      "a": "When water evaporates, only water molecules escape into the air as vapour. Salt cannot evaporate, so it stays behind and the sea stays salty.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is rain not salty?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How salty is the sea?",
      "a": "On average seawater is about 3.5 percent salt, so every litre holds about 35 grams, roughly six teaspoons. Most of it is sodium chloride, ordinary table salt.",
      "asks": [
        "How much salt is in seawater?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do volcanoes add salt to the sea?",
      "a": "Yes. Hot springs on the sea floor called hydrothermal vents, and undersea volcanoes, release minerals into the ocean, and they are a main source of the chloride in sea salt.",
      "asks": [
        "What are hydrothermal vents?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are rivers not salty?",
      "a": "Rivers do carry dissolved salts, but in tiny amounts, and the water keeps flowing away. In the sea, water leaves only by evaporation and the salt is left behind, so it builds up.",
      "asks": [
        "If rivers carry salt, why are they fresh?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Seawater averages about 35 grams of salt per litre, or 3.5 percent.",
    "Chloride and sodium make up about 85 percent of the dissolved salts in seawater.",
    "The Dead Sea is almost ten times saltier than the ocean."
  ],
